<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Zasagt Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1580 CE – 1691 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The western of the three Khalkha khanates of Outer Mongolia, whose feud with the Tüsheet Khan triggered the Dzungar invasion and Khalkha submission to the Qing.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayan-khan">Dayan Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha Mongols</a> of northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mongolia">Mongolia</a> to his youngest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geresenje">Geresenje</a>, whose sons divided them into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a>. In the late 16th century the western Khalkha under one branch of his descendants took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zasagt-khan">Zasagt Khan</a>, ruling the lands towards the Khangai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a> mountains.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khanate was a federation of noble-led nomad groups under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghisid-principle">Chinggisid</a> khan, with no fixed capital. It bordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a> and later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate</a>, and its khans also had influence on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khotogoid">Khotogoid</a> and other western groups. Like the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> it adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug Buddhism</a> in this period.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Disputes over subjects between the Zasagt and Tüsheet khans led to war in 1687, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zasagt-khan">Zasagt Khan</a> was killed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> ruler Galdan used this as a pretext to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> in 1688, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolon-nor">Dolon Nor</a> in 1691 the Khalkha nobles submitted to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>, which reorganised them as banners.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/zasagt-khanate.jpg" alt="Zasagt Khanate" width="320" height="161"><figcaption>Image: 瑞丽江的河水 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%89%8E%E8%90%A8%E5%85%8B%E5%9B%BE%E6%B1%97%E9%83%A8%E4%BD%8D%E7%BD%AE%E5%9B%BE1820.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1580 CE – 1691 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khalkha Mongolian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zasagt Khan Aimag</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1636&amp;focus=zasagt-khanate" aria-label="Zasagt Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7156 563 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zasagt Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7817 927h0M7567 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7476 819h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7476 819h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Mongolia. <a href="/atlas?year=1636&amp;focus=zasagt-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
